Below is a self-contained Rust function that one-hot-encodes all columns in a polars DataFrame that contain discrete category labels (string / categorical columns). It uses the polars crate (a modern DataFrame library for Rust). The approach:

- Detect columns whose dtype is Utf8 or Categorical.
- For each unique label in such a column, create a binary (0/1) integer column named "<origcol>_<label_sanitized>".
- For null labels, create "<origcol>_null".
- Non-categorical columns are preserved as-is.
- The implementation uses polars' lazy-expression API to build the transformed DataFrame efficiently.

Cargo.toml (add these deps; pick a 2024+ polars version as needed):
- polars = "0.36"  (adjust to the actual newest 2024+ version available)

Code:

```rust
// Cargo.toml snippet:
// [dependencies]
// polars = "0.36"   # use a 2024 or later polars release

use polars::prelude::*;

/// Sanitize a category label into a safe column-name fragment:
/// keep ASCII alphanumeric characters, replace others with '_'.
fn sanitize_label(label: &str) -> String {
    label
        .chars()
        .map(|c| if c.is_ascii_alphanumeric() { c } else { '_' })
        .collect()
}

/// One-hot encode all categorical (Utf8/Categorical) columns in `df`.
/// Returns a new DataFrame with original non-categorical columns preserved,
/// and each categorical column replaced by multiple binary (UInt8) columns,
/// one per unique label (and one for nulls if present).
///
/// Example:
///   let encoded = one_hot_encode_categories(&df)?;
pub fn one_hot_encode_categories(df: &DataFrame) -> PolarsResult<DataFrame> {
    // Collect expressions representing the new set of columns.
    let mut exprs: Vec<Expr> = Vec::with_capacity(df.width());

    for name in df.get_column_names() {
        let s = df.column(name)?;
        match s.dtype() {
            DataType::Utf8 | DataType::Categorical(_) => {
                // obtain unique values (owned Utf8Chunked)
                let utf8 = s.utf8()?;
                let uniques = utf8.unique()?;
                // iterate unique labels (Option<&str>): Some(label) or None (null)
                for opt in uniques.into_iter() {
                    match opt {
                        Some(val) => {
                            let col_name = format!("{}_{}", name, sanitize_label(val));
                            // col(name).eq(lit(val)) produces boolean; cast to UInt8 (0/1)
                            let e = col(name).eq(lit(val)).cast(DataType::UInt8).alias(&col_name);
                            exprs.push(e);
                        }
                        None => {
                            let col_name = format!("{}_null", name);
                            let e = col(name).is_null().cast(DataType::UInt8).alias(&col_name);
                            exprs.push(e);
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            // non-categorical columns: keep as-is
            _ => exprs.push(col(name)),
        }
    }

    // Build lazy plan and collect eagerly to DataFrame
    df.lazy().select(exprs).collect()
}

