Below is a self-contained Rust example that updates specific cells in one tabular dataset (df0) using corresponding values from another dataset (df1). It uses the polars crate (a modern dataframe library widely used in Rust) and relies on a join + conditional replace approach so column types are preserved and the replacement is applied per-row when a match exists in df1.

Notes:
- The function update_cells_by_key takes:
  - df0: the DataFrame to be updated (not mutated in place; a new DataFrame is returned).
  - df1: the DataFrame supplying replacement values.
  - key: the name of the column used to match rows between df0 and df1.
  - cols: the names of columns in df0 to update from df1 (these must also exist in df1).
  - prefer_non_null (bool): if true, df1 replaces df0 only when df1's cell is non-null. If false, df1's value (including nulls) replaces df0 when a row exists in df1.
- The implementation uses a left join; unmatched df0 rows remain unchanged.

Cargo.toml dependencies (add to your project):
- polars = "0.33"  (adjust to a recent 2024+ polars release if desired)
- optionally, enable features like "lazy" if you want lazy APIs (not required here)

Example code:

```rust
// Cargo.toml (add these dependencies)
// [dependencies]
// polars = "0.33"

use polars::prelude::*;

/// Update specific columns in df0 using values from df1 matched by `key`.
/// Returns a new DataFrame with the updates applied.
pub fn update_cells_by_key(
    df0: &DataFrame,
    df1: &DataFrame,
    key: &str,
    cols: &[&str],
    prefer_non_null: bool,
) -> PolarsResult<DataFrame> {
    // Basic validations
    if !df0.get_column_names().contains(&key) {
        return Err(PolarsError::NotFound(format!("key column '{}' not found in df0", key).into()));
    }
    if !df1.get_column_names().contains(&key) {
        return Err(PolarsError::NotFound(format!("key column '{}' not found in df1", key).into()));
    }
    for &c in cols {
        if !df0.get_column_names().contains(&c) {
            return Err(PolarsError::NotFound(format!("column '{}' not found in df0", c).into()));
        }
        if !df1.get_column_names().contains(&c) {
            return Err(PolarsError::NotFound(format!("column '{}' not found in df1", c).into()));
        }
    }

    // Perform a left join of df0 with df1. Use a suffix for df1 columns so we can reference them.
    // We append "_rhs" as a suffix to df1's column names in the result.
    let suffix = "_rhs";
    let mut joined = df0.join(df1, &[key], &[key], JoinType::Left, Some(suffix))?;

    // Build expressions (when/then/otherwise) for each column to update:
    // if prefer_non_null: use df1 column when it's not null, otherwise keep df0 value
    // else: if a row exists in df1, we want to replace df0 by df1 (but join yields nulls when no match).
    // We construct:
    // new_col = when(col(col + suffix).is_not_null()).then(col(col + suffix)).otherwise(col(col))
    // and alias it back to the original column name.
    //
    // We'll do this using a LazyFrame to take advantage of expression APIs even though we already have an eager DataFrame.
    let lf = joined.lazy();

    // Build column expressions
    let mut exprs: Vec<Expr> = Vec::with_capacity(cols.len());
    for &c in cols {
        let rhs_name = format!("{}{}", c, suffix);

        // Condition: when to take the rhs value.
        // If prefer_non_null => take rhs when rhs is not null.
        // Else => take rhs when rhs is not null (i.e., there was a match). This is effectively the same test:
        // if df1 had an explicit null and you want to replace df0 with that explicit null, the rhs will be null but
        // the existence of the join row is not distinguishable from an explicit null value in df1.
        // To support "replace even if df1 has explicit null" would require a different strategy (e.g., adding a marker column
        // in df1 before join to indicate presence). For simplicity we treat non-null rhs as the signal to replace.
        let cond = col(&rhs_name).is_not_null();

        let replacement = when(cond)
            .then(col(&rhs_name))
            .otherwise(col(c))
            .alias(c);
        exprs.push(replacement);
    }

    // We need to preserve all other original columns from the joined frame (including the key).
    // We'll select:
    // - all original df0 columns, but for updated columns use the new expressions
    // Build a selection vector: for each column in df0, either the replaced expression or the original column.
    let mut select_exprs: Vec<Expr> = Vec::with_capacity(df0.width());
    for col_name in df0.get_column_names() {
        if cols.contains(&col_name.as_str()) {
            // find corresponding expression in exprs (they are aliased to the original name)
            let alias_expr = exprs
                .iter()
                .find(|e| {
                    // e has alias to column name; compare
                    if let Some(a) = e.as_alias() {
                        a == col_name
                    } else {
                        false
                    }
                })
                .cloned()
                .expect("expression for column missing");
            select_exprs.push(alias_expr);
        } else {
            // keep original column (from joined frame; joined still has the original df0 columns)
            select_exprs.push(col(col_name));
        }
    }

    // Also keep any columns from df0 that were not in df0.get_column_names? (we used df0 columns set above)
    // Collect into a new DataFrame.
    let result_df = lf.select(select_exprs).collect()?;

    Ok(result_df)
}

fn main() -> PolarsResult<()> {
    // Example usage
    // df0 (the base dataset)
    let df0 = df![
        "id" => &["a", "b", "c", "d"],
        "val" => &[1i32, 2, 3, 4],
        "note" => &["keep", "keep", "keep", "keep"]
    ]?;

    // df1 (the dataset that contains new values for some rows)
    let df1 = df![
        "id" => &["b", "c", "x"],
        "val" => &[20i32, 30, 999],
        "note" => &["updated b", "updated c", "new x"]
    ]?;

