Below is a self-contained Rust function that resets the index of a tabular dataset (a Polars DataFrame) and renames that index to index_name0. It uses the polars crate (modern Polars versions from 2024+ also support the used API). The function inserts a zero-based row-index column with the requested name at the front of the DataFrame. If a column with the target name already exists, the function returns an Err to avoid accidental overwrites; you can easily change that behaviour if you prefer (see comment).

Cargo.toml (add the polars dependency):
- You can pick a modern polars release (0.31+ or newer). Example:
  polars = "0.36"  # adjust to a recent published version in your toolchain

Code:

use polars::prelude::*;

/// Reset the index of `df0` by inserting a zero-based row-index column named `index_name0`.
/// Returns a new DataFrame with the index column as the first column.
///
/// - df0: input DataFrame (consumed)
/// - index_name0: name for the new index column
/// - start: starting index value (typically 0 for zero-based)
///
/// If a column named `index_name0` already exists, this returns an Err to avoid overwriting.
/// To overwrite instead, rename or drop the existing column before calling this function.
pub fn reset_index_and_rename(
    df0: DataFrame,
    index_name0: &str,
    start: usize,
) -> PolarsResult<DataFrame> {
    // Fail if target name already exists; change this behavior if you want to overwrite
    if df0.find_idx_by_name(index_name0).is_some() {
        return Err(PolarsError::Duplicate("column with requested index name already exists".into()));
    }

    // with_row_count inserts a row-count column (index) at the front of the DataFrame.
    df0.with_row_count(index_name0, start)
}

Example usage (simple main):

fn main() -> PolarsResult<()> {
    // Example DataFrame
    let s0 = Series::new("a", &[10, 20, 30]);
    let s1 = Series::new("b", &["x", "y", "z"]);
    let df = DataFrame::new(vec![s0, s1])?;

    // Reset index and name it "index_name0"
    let df_with_index = reset_index_and_rename(df, "index_name0", 0)?;
    println!("{:?}", df_with_index);

    Ok(())
}

Notes:
- Polars does not have a distinct internal "index" concept like pandas; the common pattern is to add a row-count column using DataFrame::with_row_count.
- If you want a 1-based index, call with start = 1.
- If you prefer to overwrite an existing column with the same name, change the initial check and drop/rename that column before calling with_row_count.

