Below is a small, self-contained Rust function that sorts a list A according to the order defined by a (already-sorted) list B. Items in A that do not appear in B are placed at the beginning, and for items that do appear in B their relative order (as they appeared in A) is preserved. The routine is efficient even when B is very large: it builds one hash map from B -> rank (O(|B|) time and O(|B|) extra memory) and then processes A in a single pass (O(|A|) time), so overall O(|A| + |B|) time.

Notes:
- Generic over element type T: requires Eq + Hash + Clone.
- Stable with respect to A's order for both "missing" items and items that map to the same B element.
- Does not allocate per-B-slot vectors for every element of B — only for ranks that actually appear in A (so memory scales with number of distinct B-values seen in A, not with |B|).

Code:

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;
use std::hash::Hash;

/// Sort `a` according to the order defined by `b`.
///
/// - Elements of `a` that are not present in `b` go first (in their original relative order).
/// - Elements of `a` that are present in `b` appear after, grouped in the order specified by `b`.
///   Within each group (same element of `b`) the original relative order from `a` is preserved.
///
/// Complexity: O(|A| + |B|) time, O(|B|) additional memory for the rank map (plus O(min(|A|, |B|)) for groups).
pub fn sort_by_reference<T>(a: Vec<T>, b: &[T]) -> Vec<T>
where
    T: Eq + Hash + Clone,
{
    // Map each element in B to its index (rank).
    // If B contains duplicates we keep the first occurrence's rank.
    let mut rank: HashMap<T, usize> = HashMap::with_capacity(b.len());
    for (i, item) in b.iter().enumerate() {
        rank.entry(item.clone()).or_insert(i);
    }

    // Partition A into items not in B (missing) and items that map to some rank in B (groups).
    let mut missing: Vec<T> = Vec::new();
    let mut groups: HashMap<usize, Vec<T>> = HashMap::new();

    for item in a {
        if let Some(&i) = rank.get(&item) {
            groups.entry(i).or_insert_with(Vec::new).push(item);
        } else {
            missing.push(item);
        }
    }

    // Build result: missing items first, then groups in the order of B.
    let total_len = missing.len() + groups.values().map(|v| v.len()).sum::<usize>();
    let mut result: Vec<T> = Vec::with_capacity(total_len);
    result.extend(missing.into_iter());

    for i in 0..b.len() {
        if let Some(mut vec) = groups.remove(&i) {
            result.append(&mut vec);
        }
    }

    result
}

fn main() {
    // Example
    let a = vec!["x", "b", "a", "y", "b", "c", "a"];
    let b = vec!["a", "b", "c", "d"]; // desired order for elements that appear in B

    let sorted = sort_by_reference(a.into_iter().map(String::from).collect(), &b.iter().map(|s| s.to_string()).collect::<Vec<_>>());
    // Expected result:
    // - Items not in B: "x", "y"  (in original order)
    // - Then items in order of B:
    //   - "a" occurrences from A in original order: "a", "a"
    //   - "b" occurrences: "b", "b"
    //   - "c" occurrences: "c"
    println!("{:?}", sorted);
}
```

If you want even faster hashing for very large B (or for adversarial inputs), you can replace the std HashMap with a faster hasher (for example the ahash crate) by changing the map type — but the algorithm structure remains the same.

