Below is a small, self-contained Rust function that uses the modern Polars DataFrame library to replace missing values (nulls) in one column with the corresponding values from another column. It uses Polars' lazy expressions and the coalesce expression so it works for any column type (it will attempt to cast the source column to the target column's dtype if they differ).

Cargo.toml (add the polars dependency; pick a 2024+ polars version you prefer):
- Example:
  polars = { version = "0.34", features = ["lazy"] }

Function and example usage:

```rust
use polars::prelude::*;

/// Replace null/missing values in `target` column by corresponding values from `source` column.
/// Returns a new DataFrame with the replacement applied.
///
/// - If either column name is not present, returns an error.
/// - If dtypes differ, attempts to cast `source` to the dtype of `target`. If that fails,
///   an error is returned.
///
/// Usage:
/// let df = replace_missing_with_other(df, "col_with_nulls", "backup_col")?;
pub fn replace_missing_with_other(
    df: DataFrame,
    target: &str,
    source: &str,
) -> PolarsResult<DataFrame> {
    // Ensure columns exist and get dtypes
    let target_dtype = df.column(target)?.dtype().clone();
    let source_dtype = df.column(source)?.dtype().clone();

    // Build a source expression; cast if necessary
    let source_expr = if source_dtype != target_dtype {
        // Attempt to cast source to target dtype in the expression
        col(source).cast(target_dtype.clone())
    } else {
        col(source)
    };

    // Build expressions: replace target by coalesce(target, source_casted)
    let exprs: Vec<Expr> = df
        .get_column_names()
        .iter()
        .map(|name| {
            if name == target {
                // coalesce: take target when not null, otherwise take source_expr
                col(name).coalesce(&[source_expr.clone()]).alias(name)
            } else {
                col(name)
            }
        })
        .collect();

    // Use lazy frame select for an efficient column-wise transform
    let out = df.lazy().select(exprs).collect()?;
    Ok(out)
}

fn main() -> PolarsResult<()> {
    // Example: create a DataFrame with some nulls
    let s1 = Series::new("a", &[Some(1i64), None, Some(3), None]);
    let s2 = Series::new("b", &[Some(10i64), Some(20), None, Some(40)]);
    let df = DataFrame::new(vec![s1, s2])?;

    println!("Before:\n{}", &df);

    let filled = replace_missing_with_other(df, "a", "b")?;

    println!("After:\n{}", &filled);
    Ok(())
}
```

Notes:
- This uses Polars' lazy expression col(...).coalesce([...]) which picks the first non-null value across expressions for each row.
- If the source and target column types differ, the function attempts to cast the source column to the target type (so e.g., strings can be cast to numbers if parsable). If casting cannot succeed at runtime, Polars will return an error.
- The function returns a new DataFrame; the original is not modified in place. If you want in-place mutation, you can assign the returned DataFrame back to your variable.

